WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.568
-Yeah, it's so great
to see you guys.

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:04.103
Come on. Hello.

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:05.571
-Hi, Jimmy.

00:00:05.571 --> 00:00:07.273
-You're here on a perfect day.

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.809
It is Global James Bond Day.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:13.179
And you both wrote and recorded
the theme song,

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.513
which is awesome.

00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:15.781
I love it.

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:17.050
I want to know everything.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.752
I want to know
how it all came together.

00:00:18.752 --> 00:00:21.721
I want to know every single --
just go for like two hours.

00:00:21.721 --> 00:00:24.524
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:28.561
-Yeah, it was a crazy,
surreal experience.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:31.631
I don't remember
when it all kind of...

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:33.533
-Started.
-...was real.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.602
But I do know that
for many years

00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.973
we've wanted to
write a Bond song.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:41.641
Not even like we
thought we could.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:43.442
It was just more like...
-A fantasy.

00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:45.912
-...a fantasy,
like a complete fantasy.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:48.914
Like, how cool would it be
to do this, you know?

00:00:48.914 --> 00:00:53.186
But I think it was
last summer-ish.

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:57.456
It started being a topic of
discussion and not a fantasy,

00:00:57.456 --> 00:01:02.462
and it was very insane to us.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.964
Yeah, it was --
it was a couple months of

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:08.634
very, like, disbelief, I think.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:11.237
-Were there scraps of songs
that you tried and you're like,

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:13.406
"I think I got it.
This is 'No Time to Die.'"

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.174
And you're like...
♪ No time to die ♪

00:01:15.174 --> 00:01:17.977
♪ Da-ba-bup, ba-ba-bup,
no time to die, ooh ♪

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.312
And then, you go,
"Okay, let me just come back --

00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:21.948
Let's do this tomorrow."

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:25.351
Or did you know immediately,
like, "Oh, this is the tune"?

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:29.055
-You know, what's funny is
that we kind of started

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:33.159
with making sure we got,
like, a melody that we felt

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:36.162
was really solid before we tried
any lyrics out.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:40.500
But we did definitely, you know,
before we had written anything,

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.737
go like, I -- we both think
it's really important to have

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:47.373
the title of the movie
in the song

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.976
and have the song be called that
because it just --

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.645
it wouldn't have been satisfying
otherwise, and so --

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.913
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:56.315
-Like, thinking how would
we make that make sense?

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:59.118
What could we say?
What could we -- Whatever.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:02.322
So it was kind of -- we were
kind of stuck for like a few --

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:04.023
the first few days
we tried to work on it.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.958
We were, like, you know --
It was a lot of pressure.

00:02:05.958 --> 00:02:07.359
It's a [bleep] Bond song.

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:10.863
It's like, what are you supposed
to -- So we were pretty stumped,

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:13.967
and then there was just one day
where Finneas was playing on

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.802
the piano, and he just played
this one melody,

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.803
and it was just that...
♪ Da-da-da ♪

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.071
And we were just like....

00:02:19.071 --> 00:02:20.439
-"Yes!"

00:02:20.439 --> 00:02:22.141
It's like, dude,
it's so perfect.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:25.078
You go -- Because they all
have that, like, mysterious --

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:27.680
They have, like, a mystique
to the Bond themes.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.048
It was like...
-Yeah.

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:32.518
-It's like...
♪ Da-da-da, da ♪

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:36.756
It's weird progression,
but then you got that --

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.758
Oh, and it's almost
like that one --

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.326
I don't even know
what chord it is.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.061
But it's like that...
♪ Rrrnggg ♪

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.496
-Yeah.

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:45.063
-You know, with
the jangly guitar.

00:02:45.063 --> 00:02:46.666
And you're like, "Ah."

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:47.967
I don't know.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.702
Did they like it as soon as
you sent it to them?

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:51.237
Did they have notes?

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.771
-Hans Zimmer loved it.
I know that.

00:02:52.771 --> 00:02:56.075
He was the one, I know that --
He really connected to it,

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.743
which was crazy for us.

00:02:57.743 --> 00:02:59.078
-Hans Zimmer?

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.813
-Yeah, I know.
-The greatest.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.682
-How was that whole thing?

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.550
-It was crazy!

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.552
It was -- He is a doll.

00:03:06.552 --> 00:03:08.687
He is such a joy.

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:14.426
And his main thing was that he
wanted us to just see the movie

00:03:14.426 --> 00:03:18.264
before we --
like, just so that we knew.

00:03:18.264 --> 00:03:21.166
So we flew out to London,
and we watched the movie,

00:03:21.166 --> 00:03:25.137
which was, you know,
like a rough cut, I guess,

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:28.441
a year ago-ish,
under a year ago.

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:32.178
And then we worked with him
for like a week-ish.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.613
-Had an orchestra.
It was really cool.

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.648
-It was really amazing.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.049
-He's so cool.
-Yeah.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:38.651
-I mean, how fun is that?

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.085
How does it work with
an orchestra?

00:03:40.085 --> 00:03:43.356
Like, can you change things
or they just kind of know

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:45.057
what's already written and they
go, "We're just doing this"?

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:48.595
-Dude, that's a good question,
and I would have thought that

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:51.363
you couldn't change stuff once
it was written, but all day,

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:52.699
Hans, Stephen Lipson,

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.633
who also helped
produced the song with us,

00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:56.269
and we would lean in
and we'd go like,

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.903
"Could we have them try

00:03:57.903 --> 00:03:59.438
something a little bit spookier
here?"

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:01.941
And they would go to the
orchestra and say, "Do this."

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.009
And they would change
in real time.

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.743
-It was crazy.
-Like, all 70 people.

00:04:04.743 --> 00:04:07.179
Everyone was so good.
It was incredible.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.414
-That's pretty cool.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:12.318
You both did a virtual
performance at the DNC.

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:15.187
I think it was "My Future,"
and it was great.

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.690
Everyone looked -- People were
like, "This was so good."

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.694
And before you performed,
Billie, you spoke.

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.696
Why was that important to you?

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:27.800
-To be honest, I just think

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.169
it was my responsibility
to do that.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:37.310
I really couldn't want to be
involved in politics less.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:40.413
I would literally rather do
anything else

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:43.482
than be talking about this
and be doing anything.

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:48.755
And I just think
that that's not...

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:50.656
I think that should show you

00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:53.459
how much this actually
means right now

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:57.630
because even with me,
who is somebody that, like,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.266
I used to just, you know,
completely shut up.

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:01.801
I don't want to talk about it.
I don't want to deal with it.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.135
I don't want to
hear anybody's opinions.

00:05:03.135 --> 00:05:04.737
I don't want to
give my own opinions.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.371
You know, I don't want to
deal with it.

00:05:06.371 --> 00:05:07.840
I don't want to think about it.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.575
There just comes a certain point
where, like,

00:05:09.575 --> 00:05:11.277
you can't just --
-Ignore it.

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.312
-Yeah, you can't ignore it.
You can't be silent.

00:05:13.312 --> 00:05:16.849
And I think it's -- especially
having the platform that I have,

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.284
I don't want to waste that.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:23.689
So I think it's really important
that we all talk and speak up

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.692
even if we'd rather die.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:28.193
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:05:28.193 --> 00:05:30.663
-I think it was really cool
that you did that.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.832
Finneas, last time you were
on the show, we talked about

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:37.570
how you and Billie work everyday
sounds into your songs.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:41.574
You actually played me, like, a
crosswalk beeping or something.

00:05:41.574 --> 00:05:44.877
It was so --
It was mind-blowing.

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:47.879
I was wondering
if you wouldn't mind showing us

00:05:47.879 --> 00:05:49.649
how you do some of it.

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:50.950
-Absolutely.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.853
So, thank you
for segueing into this.

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:56.622
And thank you also for being
generous enough to send me

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.124
your four recordings
that you sent.

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:02.661
Just for the audience, I'm going
to play a couple of them.

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:04.696
This is my personal
favorite one.

00:06:04.696 --> 00:06:05.798
♪ -Jimmy Bond ♪

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:06.832
-I like that one a lot.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:09.101
-Oh, my gosh. Jimmy Bond.

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:10.436
-I like Jimmy Bond a lot.
-Please don't laugh at me.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:12.504
Please. Jimmy Bond.

00:06:12.504 --> 00:06:13.773
-Yeah, that one's great.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:14.807
♪ -Vote ♪

00:06:14.807 --> 00:06:16.175
